The noise sensitivity of a quantum computer is proportional
to the spatial and temporal extent over which it is
required to maintain phase coherence.  Thus if the time
required to perform the algorithm only increases
polynomially fast with the number of bits, then the noise
sensitivity only increases polynomially fast with the
number of bits, hence we can legitimately ignore noise.
Noise will be a very large problem, but it is a problem of
order P(m), not of order 2^m or of order m!, where m is the
number of bits.  (The time required for a classical
computer to perform the simulation of course does increase
exponentially with the number of bits - m^3 * 2^m, but the
quantum computer being simulated continues to take a
reasonably fast time.

By the uncertainty principle, the noise energy required to
disturb a quantum computer is inversely proportional to
how long the computation takes.

