Technological change was very visible in the twelfth
century, and in the sixteenth century (handguns, etc), and
people commented on the acceleration of technological
change, then as now. It has been more visible in the
nineteenth and twentieth century.  

The change in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries is
seems larger to us, but is this merely a perspective
effect?

Is the difference between the AK47 and the handgun greater
than the difference between the longbow and the handgun?
I doubt it. On the other hand the difference between the
airplane and the stirrup, *is* greater than the difference
between the stirrup and the war chariot.

There was radical change between 1920 and 1950, but no
radical change between 1950 and 1980.

The growth in energy consumption per head in America was
more rapid between 1850 and 1920, than it was between 1920
and 1990. (Historical Statistics of the United States,
Colonial times to 1970, issued by the Bureau of the Census,
Washington DC)

Although there has been a general trend of acceleration,
there have also been large fluctuations in this trend
caused by social, political and cultural changes.

Technology is once again accelerating, and it looks like
we are in for another period of rapid technological change,
but I doubt that the coming wave is the start of
singularity.  I expect it will be just another wave. (I
hope it will be the singularity of course, but before we
hit the singularity we will have to understand the problems
involved in creating artificial intelligence.  Despite
numerous reports to the contrary "Mind is still a mystery,
and Elvis is still dead."

Between quantum mechanics and computers on every desk, the
delay was about seventy five years.  Between Newtonian
physics, and rockets to the moon, the delay was about three
hundred years.

I expect a very substantial delay between the scientific
breakthrough that makes it possible for us to start working
on artificial intelligence, and the technology that makes
it possible to expand intelligence to a useful extent.

