Subject: META: A Proposal for Easy Ratings of Articles and Authors

I propose here a simple system for rating postings, with the intent
that the currently moribund "Essay" list be replaced by a low-volume
"Best of Extropians" list. I also have some comments about the
stagnant HEx market.

(Three topics in one post, but they're related.)

At a mostly Extropians party last night (Amara and Vince), there was
some discussion of rating postings and, in a different conversation,
of the Essay list. Some points emerged:

* some people seem to think the HEx market is mainly designed to
"rate" posts and posters. I think this is misguided, as most markets
operate to make money (thornes), e.g., I buy and sell, when I do at
all, based on what I think the future price will be, not based on
giving approval or demerits to posters. Some people even add notices
with things like: "If you liked this post, buy Foo on HEx." (The
analogous situation might be signs at MacDonalds saying: "If you liked
this Big Mac, buy MacDonald's stock." Right.)

* all agreed that HEx is currently so thinly traded as to
uninteresting at this time. (What to do about this is a topic for
another time.)

* one non-member of the list is on the Essay list and commented on the
low volume of it. (This was a reminder to me that the Essay list even
exists....I'd pretty much forgotten about it.)

So, HEx is hardly suited for rating posts or individul posters. And
the Essay list is very low-volume. (The naive solution to the Essay
list volume problem, assuming one post ever several weeks represents
a "problem," is to encourage more folks to write essays for it. This
fails for many reasons, mostly having to do with the spontaneity of
posts to the list and the unwillingness of many of us to polish such
pieces for the Essay list. Also, the lack of feedback, of discussion,
etc., makes the Essay list even more of the "isolated speeches"
environment Hal Finney wrote about recently. Thus, urging more essays
has not and likely will not work.)

Here's my proposal:

* use the current "::command" syntax in the following way:

- upon reading a post, a reader may send back a command of the form
"::rating", where "rating" is a number between 0 and 10.

- thus, suppose I am reading a post entitled "How I Froze My Assets
Off by Investing in Cryonics," by Isaac Berg. I decide this is a great
article, so I respond to it by sending the command:

"::8"

- I presume the List software could be modified (I'm making no claims
as to how easy this will be, or if should be done this way...just
proposing and idea to talk about) so as to apply this rating to both
the specific article, the author, and even perhaps a cumulative
measure of the popularity of the thread itself.

(If the List software has to have the reader type something more
verbose, like "::rate article 1093225 8" then of course it will be
much more cumbersome to use and may not be used by many people. I
would favor the simple command described above, with the List software
handling the pointers.)

* There are still flaws in this system. For one thing, the
"popularity contest" nature...but this is what we tried with HEx, so
nothing is new. And the reputations or popularites of the raters are
not reflected in the vote, unless even more complicated software is
developed. (Which may be fine for a second-generation system, but for
now the simple "::8" system should be tried.

Another issue is who votes? True of all voluntary systems.

Other flaws in all such schemes exist, and we can discuss them if
there's interest.

* Here's where it gets interesting:

- if the voting is done fairly quickly (as with the easy-reply
scheme), then posts can be rated in time for the evening digest, for
example. Thus, Alice could request that she be sent only those posts
which got an average score of 5 or better.

- or only see posts from posters whose rating is, say, 4 or higher.
And so on.

- this effectively allows the readers to set a threshold for "best of"
posts, given the limitations (there has to be a time delay, of course,
and at least some readers have to be getting the "raw" feed...if
everyone is setting a threshold, the problems are obvious).

* The ratings of individual posts can also go into a running
score---perhaps exponentially smoothed with a time constant of a month
or so so as to give more weight to recent posts--for the poster
himself. And even the thread itself, though this seems much less
important (since threads change name so often).

One might imagine issuing a command and seeing this report:

RATINGS REPORT

Article: 109347564, "How I Froze My Assets Off by Investing in
Cryonics," Isaac Berg. Rated 6.92 by 23 respondents.

Rating of Isaac Berg: 4.91


(Pay no attention to the precise form of the report, as that's up to
the developers, input from others, etc.)


* With this kind of reader feedback, filtering commands could be made
even more useful. The articles most enjoyable to the most people could
then be automatically collected as a substitute for the Essay list, or
as supplemental kind of low-volume list.

* Like I said, some downsides. Some people will get their feelings
hurt, some people will get angry. Such is life.

I'd rather have some form of real market in postings, as with the way
we vote with our dollars in deciding what magazines and books to buy,
but I suspect the Extropians list cannot reasonably solve this tough
problem in the near term. (It's not just the lack of suitable
software, it's the natural unwillingness to run everything as a
market...after all, we don't have conversations in which participants
buy and sell parts of it, do we?).

Someday it may be plausible for each of us to have a weighted voting
scheme, where the ratings are themselves weighted by our own respect
for the voter. (This would involve the List software storing a vector
or table of weightings for each user and doing some crunching. Not a
thing to consider lightly.)

I claim that this very simple "::rating" addition to our List software
would open up new avenues for filtering messagess based on the
recommendations of others. It may sound like a "herd mentality," but
it reflects how many of us pay attention to the popularity of books,
magazines, movies, etc. 

At least it's a start.

--Tim

