From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Beating the stock market

Perry Metzger:
> Nick, look in the last Forbes 400 at the profile of Warren Buffet, the
> richest man in America. He made every penny he's earned from
> fundamentals based investing.

Based on his knowledge of technology?  I don't think so.
Fundamental analysis typically refers foremost to looking at
the balance sheet, and secondarily at a wide variety of econometric
factors affecting the company's business, and at company
attributes like management team, marketing of the company's products,
and on occasion technology analysis.  The comments in my last
message were focused on informal technology analysis as had been mentioned 
by yourself and several others on the list.  Which I'm not against,
but I think the way it is done could be greatly improved.

> if you are always completely
> in hedge, you are going to cut your returns dramatically.

You'll cut your _variance_ in returns dramatically. You'll win
less, lose less.  That's why I said hedge, not all the risk, but
as much as possible of the risk not associated with the information
derived from technology analysis.  BTW, if the trader you are
talking about is Paul Tudor Jones, part of his claimed "edge" is his
ability to track overall short-term econometric trends ("time the
market"), and engage in voluminous trade in close connection
with floor traders, getting a tiny piece of the bid/ask spreads
(probably the main source of his edge).  Both
strategies argue against hedging market risk -- for him such
hedging would be a counter-bet on the very thing he claims to
have an edge at, it would rack up large transaction costs with 
his voluminous trading, and put restrictions on the split-second timing 
of trades he needs.  The buy-and-hold technology investor in constrast 
greatly profits from such hedges.

> but if you are betting that Kodak is going to recover, completely
> hedging your position with, say, futures, means that should the
> economy do better you are also completely hedged against making any
> money. 

My point is, this is a suboptimal bet to be making based on the claimed
information.  The proposition is that you have unique and important 
information that, say, Kodak's technology is promising, not that you 
have some supernatural oracle which tells you "Kodak will recover".  
With this strategy you would needlessly have lost quite a bit of 
money in many cases (90% in 1929-32, 60% after inflation in 1970-1982, 
etc.), for reasons unlrelated to the technology.  Of course you would 
have made money in 1982-92, again for reasons unrelated to that specific
technology, so some contemporary traders might falsely believe it.
If you have unique info that Kodak's technology is 
better than its competitors, this says nothing about how the overall 
economy is going to do.  You shouldn't be betting for or against the
expected growth rate, there are $100's of billions worth of investors with 
vastly more information on that than you, it's not not your edge and so 
you're needlessly throwing darts at the board, putting your money at risk.  
(Or worse, trading on old news against the smart money which has already
adjusted the price to reflect the news).  As as a practical matter, alas, 
most people can't or don't know how to construct such a hedge and end up 
doing the riskier investment.

> Well, actually, there is plenty to be computerized in fundamentals
> analysis,

Which I'm all in favor of, but that wasn't the original point I
was responding to.

> Since this sort of thing would go away if
> everyone did it, I'm not talking about what I think the strategy would
> be untill someone throws me the cash.

